pr_comments:
So, let's see a show of hands. 

Who would like to see this logic implemented in the RF core?

![tx-call-graph](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/983494/93635050-9c071100-f9bf-11ea-9f21-11da6ca3cbbe.png)

The advantage is that we eliminate the `fg_done` semaphore so that TX logic is not bouncing between the M0 (RF core) and the M4 (System core). Eliminating that would probably make TX about as performant as it can get, and also give more cycles to the system core.

Putting it in the RF core is just a bit of extra legwork, but if people really want that feature here, I could probably add it to this PR. Otherwise, I'm fine leaving it for a later optimization.

@bwitherspoon
@jukkar
@vanti
@tbursztyka

It was a simple fix - good find!!

```diff
diff --git a/drivers/ieee802154/ieee802154_cc13xx_cc26xx.c b/drivers/ieee802154/ieee802154_cc13xx_cc26xx.c
index 8f68b701d8..04ea717b34 100644
--- a/drivers/ieee802154/ieee802154_cc13xx_cc26xx.c
+++ b/drivers/ieee802154/ieee802154_cc13xx_cc26xx.c
@@ -274,7 +274,7 @@ static int ieee802154_cc13xx_cc26xx_tx(const struct device *dev,
                reason = RF_runScheduleCmd(drv_data->rf_handle,
                        (RF_Op *)&drv_data->cmd_ieee_csma, &sched_params,
                        cmd_ieee_csma_callback, RF_EventLastFGCmdDone);
-               if ((reason & RF_EventLastCmdDone) == 0) {
+               if ((reason & RF_EventLastFGCmdDone) == 0) {
                        LOG_DBG("Failed to run command (0x%" PRIx64 ")",
                                reason);
                        continue;
```

@vanti - I discovered what was stopping the show today.

The RF synth just goes to sleep after a certain amount of time. I have a workaround in my SubGHz branch for detecting and correcting this on the TX side. However, it seems to happen on the RX side as well.

I'll contact TI to see if there is possibly some way that we can subscribe to a notification or interrupt saying that the synth has powered-down.

comments:
@SML892 - yes, that is (some of) the driver code performing modulo arithmetic, and I am well aware of it's existence and how modulo arithmetic works.

The actual bug is slightly more nuanced than that. Increasing the modulus would not solve the actual problem. I am already working with @vanti and others at TI to solve the bug in the SimpleLink SDK).

The information is provided here simply  to demonstrate that there is a work-around and to un-block several other pieces of radio related work that depend on using TI's RF driver API (adding support for Sub GHz, RAW mode for 2.4 GHz, RAW mode for Sub GHz, simultaneous BLE support, ..).

